WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.438
Thermal throttling reduces clocks when hardware approaches temperature or power limits.

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:10.441
A system may start quickly and slow after several minutes.

00:00:10.441 --> 00:00:19.359
Monitor sustained temperature and clock behavior, clean obstructed airflow, and verify that desktop intake and exhaust paths are balanced.

00:00:19.359 --> 00:00:25.232
More aggressive fan curves may improve clocks at the cost of noise.

00:00:25.232 --> 00:00:30.670
Optimize for a sustainable operating point rather than a short burst.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:39.154
Laptops require special attention to power mode, charger capability, surface airflow, and shared cooling between CPU and GPU.

00:00:39.154 --> 00:00:44.157
Maximum-performance mode can increase heat enough to reduce long-run efficiency.

00:00:44.157 --> 00:00:48.725
Compare balanced and high-power modes on the complete workload.

00:00:48.725 --> 00:00:57.643
On desktops, unstable overclocks, undersized power supplies, and poor case ventilation can appear as model crashes or device resets.

00:00:57.643 --> 00:01:02.646
Memory pressure causes allocation failures, heavy swapping, or abrupt termination.

00:01:02.646 --> 00:01:09.389
Reduce context, batch, offload, or model size before treating crashes as random software defects.

00:01:09.389 --> 00:01:14.827
Close unnecessary GPU applications and control background services during critical runs.

00:01:14.827 --> 00:01:23.746
Manage model caches so storage does not fill unexpectedly, and retain enough free space for downloads and temporary files.

00:01:23.746 --> 00:01:27.008
Apply driver and runtime updates deliberately.

00:01:27.008 --> 00:01:33.317
Record known-good versions, preserve configuration, and test one representative model after a change.

00:01:33.317 --> 00:01:38.755
Keep a rollback path when new software changes performance or compatibility.

00:01:38.755 --> 00:01:49.848
Operational discipline turns a tuned experiment into a dependable tool: predictable startup, controlled resources, observable failure, and recovery without rebuilding the environment from memory.
